  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45125,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45125.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, we've had a long tradition and a lot of our traditions and our Constitution and Bill of Rights actually come from England for hundreds of years before. So really, there's a long tradition to, if someone accuses you of your crime, they can't do it anonymously. Now, a long time ago, there were societies like in Venice in the 15th century. You know, if you go to visit Venice, you'll see like a lion's mouth and you can put gossip in there and get your neighbor convicted of things through gossip and hearsay and anonymous accusations. But really, in England and then in the United States, we didn't have that. In fact, we put in the Sixth Amendment specifically that you have the right to confront your accusers. Now, the Democrats will say, ah, it's not really a trial. It's just impeachment. He doesn't get that right. But an impeachment is a lot like a trial. In fact, the Democrats in the next breath tell us we should be treating it like a trial. And really, the president is accused of high crimes and misdemeanors. That's what the impeachment is about. Well, that sounds like criminal accusation. To me, I think you should be able to confront your accuser. They argue the whistleblower statute says, oh, you can't name this person. That's not actually true. The whistleblower statute protects you from being fired, and it protects you from having any kind of legal recourse against you But it doesn't say that you remain anonymous if there's a legal proceeding. In fact, most people believe that there is any legal proceeding, and the whistleblower is the one giving evidence to have to come forward. In this case, I think it's incredibly important because it's basically the whistleblower's opinion. I mean, he got two dozen other people listening to the phone call who don't believe there was anything inappropriate. The whistleblower thought there was, and yet the whistleblower never even heard the phone call. He's like hearing gossip around the coffee cooler. uh... the water cooler he's hearing gossip and then so i'm going to become a whistleblower bring down the president i think most people think that that's kind of unfair and that the president's not being treated fairly in the fifth impeachment proceedings been kind of a kangaroo court so i think in the end it comes down just pure partisanship",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The U.S. has inherited many traditions, including its Constitution and Bill of Rights, from England.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The Sixth Amendment guarantees the right to confront one's accusers.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The whistleblower’s claim is based on secondhand information rather than direct observation.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "England and the United States historically rejected anonymous accusations in legal matters.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The whistleblower did not personally hear the phone call in question.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The President is accused of high crimes and misdemeanors during impeachment.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Whistleblowers are protected from retaliation but may still need to testify publicly.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Impeachment resembles a criminal trial in key respects.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The whistleblower statute does not require anonymity in legal proceedings.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Multiple individuals who listened to the phone call did not find anything improper.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Legal fairness requires personal confrontation with accusers in serious allegations.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "A fair process must allow the accused to challenge the credibility of their accuser directly.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Anonymity in formal proceedings undermines due process and justice.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Personal experience is more credible than hearsay when assessing misconduct.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Public trust in institutions depends on transparency and accountability in accusations.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Anonymous accusations are inherently unreliable and prone to abuse.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The legitimacy of impeachment hinges on treating it like a real trial.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Whistleblower protections are intended to encourage truthful testimony, not shield identities permanently.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "High-level officials deserve the same evidentiary standards applied in criminal cases.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "Partisan motives invalidate procedural fairness in political processes.",
